<!--
    kuna GH-1650 `repstring`: SLEIGH lifts an x86 repeat prefix as a loop
    inside the one instruction, so an inlined copy, fill or compare printed as
    `while (n) { *p = *q; n -= 1; ... }` or
    `do { if (!n) return 1; ... } while (x == y);`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P3 "simplification-quiescence".  The P0 assertion
    `option repstring on|off` decides whether the proved loop becomes
    `builtin_memcpy`, `builtin_memset`, `builtin_memset32` or `builtin_memcmp`.

    The bytes are .rodata and .text of decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/
    fixtures/repstring_x86_64, built from repstring.c by gcc 15.3
    (-Os -minline-all-stringops -fno-pic): fill32 (`rep stosd` of a word
    whose bytes differ), zero32 (of zero), fill8 (`rep stosb`), copy32 and
    copy_end (`rep movsd`, the second returning the final destination), isdash
    (`strcmp(s, "-") == 0` inlined as `repz cmpsb`), order and below (a
    `memcmp` result built from the carry and zero flags), same_n (a run-time
    length), same5 (no flags set up before the instruction) and mismatch (the
    final pointer is returned, so it is no memcmp).

    zero_g (0x201400) is not from the fixture: it is gcc 15.3's
    `-O2 -mstringop-strategy=rep_8byte` code for
    `char gfill[128]; void zero_g(void) { memset(gfill, 0, 128); gfill[5] = 'x'; }`
    linked at 0x201400 with gfill at 0x203000, which `map address` names; the
    fill's destination is the global, so it prints as `gfill`.

    Pass 1 runs with no option line, so it pins the shipped default.  Pass 2
    (`option repstring off`) reproduces the bug: every instruction is a loop.
-->
